{"id": "1683010719", "uploader": "Ocean Networks Canada", "uploader_id": "65324226", "uploader_url": "https://soundcloud.com/oceannetworkscanada", "timestamp": 1701797117, "title": "Ice Breaking up in Cambridge Bay", "track": "Ice Breaking up in Cambridge Bay", "description": "This clip was recorded on the day in early July 2019, when continuous sea ice melted and broke apart. Ice usually begins growing in October and continues getting thicker until late June, when weather turns warm and the ice melts rapidly. In 2019, the ice was about 2 m thick in early June, then it gradually melted to a thickness of about 75 cm on July 1st 2019. On July 2nd, the thickness dropped to zero. We might imagine the prominent sound in this clip to be ice cracking and turning over at the sea surface.\n\nBelow is a still frame from the video time lapse on that day. You can see the water in the bay is a mixture of open water and floating ice. You can watch the full day's time lapse video here: https://data.oceannetworks.ca/SeaTube?resourceTypeId=1000&resourceId=526&diveId=72284900&time=2019-07-01T00:00:53.000Z", "thumbnails": [{"id": "mini", "url": "https://i1.sndcdn.com/artworks-r8xnpsvx51fhpjPb-cxMyvQ-mini.jpg", "width": 16, "height": 16, "resolution": "16x16"}, {"id": "tiny", "url": "https://i1.sndcdn.com/artworks-r8xnpsvx51fhpjPb-cxMyvQ-tiny.jpg", "width": 20, "height": 20, "resolution": "20x20"}, {"id": "small", "url": "https://i1.sndcdn.com/artworks-r8xnpsvx51fhpjPb-cxMyvQ-small.jpg", "width": 32, "height": 32, "resolution": "32x32"}, {"id": "badge", "url": "https://i1.sndcdn.com/artworks-r8xnpsvx51fhpjPb-cxMyvQ-badge.jpg", "width": 47, "height": 47, "resolution": "47x47"}, {"id": "t67x67", "url": "https://i1.sndcdn.com/artworks-r8xnpsvx51fhpjPb-cxMyvQ-t67x67.jpg", "width": 67, "height": 67, "resolution": "67x67"}, {"id": "large", "url": "https://i1.sndcdn.com/artworks-r8xnpsvx51fhpjPb-cxMyvQ-large.jpg", "width": 100, "height": 100, "resolution": "100x100"}, {"id": "t300x300", "url"